{"builder":"Goal: when a config file has malformed JSON, the error names the file path and the line and column of the mistake. The app must still fail with exit 1. Do not make `loadConfig` swallow the error or return `{}`/defaults. That was the original suggested fix and it is rejected.\n\nCurrent code:\n- `src/config.js`: `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`.\n- `src/app.js`: catches any error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1. Otherwise it prints `listening on <port>`.\n- `test/config.test.js`: has two tests, 'reads a config' and 'bad JSON throws' (the second asserts a SyntaxError). `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, wrap the `JSON.parse` call in a try/catch that catches only the parse error. Rethrow an error whose `message` contains:\n   - the `path` exactly as passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path)\n   - the line and column of the mistake, for example `<path>: line 3 column 3: <original message>` or `<path>:3:3: ...`\n   Compute the line and column yourself rather than relying only on Node's message, because some Node versions give only `position N` or no location at all:\n   - If the message has `line L column C`, use those numbers.\n   - Otherwise take `position N` and count newlines in the text before offset N. Line and column are both 1-based.\n   - Make sure the 4-line sample below comes out as line 3, column 3.\n   Keep the thrown error a `SyntaxError` (e.g. `new SyntaxError(msg, { cause: err })`) so the existing 'bad JSON throws' test still passes. Leave read errors such as ENOENT as they are. Do not change the return value for valid JSON.\n2. Leave `src/app.js` printing `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exiting 1. Nothing may be written to stdout on failure.\n3. Add tests to `test/config.test.js` that fail against the current `src/`:\n   - `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws, and the error's `message` includes that file's path.\n   - For a file containing the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, the message includes the path and identifies line 3 and column 3.\n   Keep the existing tests passing.\n4. Optionally update the README sentence about `bad config: <message>` and exit 1 to mention the file path and location. Keep the documented behaviour.\n5. Check your work with:\n   - `npm test`\n   - `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > \"$d/c.json\"; node src/app.js \"$d/c.json\"; echo \"exit $?\"`. Expect exit 1, no stdout, and stderr with `bad config:`, the path `$d/c.json`, and line 3, column 3.\n   - `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > \"$d/ok.json\"; node src/app.js \"$d/ok.json\"; echo \"exit $?\"`. Expect `listening on 9000` and exit 0.\n\nOut of scope: changing how a missing file is reported (beyond it staying an exit-1 `bad config:` failure), validating config values or schema, and supporting comments or JSON5.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head from the repository root, using the repo's Node version (v22). Check 5 needs BASE, the commit the PR targets (its merge-base with the target). Set it first, e.g. `BASE=$(git merge-base HEAD <target>)`.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > \"$d/c.json\"; node src/app.js \"$d/c.json\" >\"$d/out\" 2>\"$d/err\"; echo \"exit $?\"; echo \"stdout bytes: $(wc -c <\"$d/out\")\"; cat \"$d/err\"; echo \"D=$d\"`\nPASS: it prints `exit 1` and `stdout bytes: 0`.\nFAIL: any other exit code, or any stdout output (such as `listening on`).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nUse the stderr printed by check 1 (the `cat \"$d/err\"` output; the D= line shows the directory).\nPASS: stderr contains all of:\n- `bad config:`\n- the exact string `<D>/c.json`\n- a location giving line 3 and column 3, e.g. `line 3 column 3` or `3:3`\nFAIL: any of these is missing, the path is altered (e.g. resolved differently), or the location is wrong.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/b.json\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"; echo \"exit $?\"`\nPASS: it prints `OK`.\nFAIL: it prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > \"$d/ok.json\"; node src/app.js \"$d/ok.json\"; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, { port: 9000 }); console.log('OK', JSON.stringify(v))\"`\nPASS: stdout shows `listening on 9000`, then `exit 0`, then `OK {\"port\":9000}`.\nFAIL: anything else, such as a different port, a non-zero exit, or an assertion error.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nStep 1: run `npm test` at the PR head. It must report `# fail 0` and exit 0.\nStep 2: run the PR's tests against the base source:\n`w=$(mktemp -d); git worktree add --detach \"$w\" \"$BASE\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && rm -rf \"$w/test\" && cp -r test \"$w/test\" && (cd \"$w\" && npm test 2>&1 | grep -E '^(not ok|# fail)'); git worktree remove --force \"$w\"`\nThen read the failing test(s) in `test/`.\nPASS: step 1 passes, and step 2 shows at least one `not ok` test that asserts the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\nFAIL: `npm test` fails at head, nothing fails against base, or no failing test checks the path.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Read `src/config.js` and `src/app.js` to see whether the line and column are computed from the text or a position, rather than relying only on Node's own message (other Node versions may omit them).\n- Note whether a missing-file case still exits 1 with `bad config:`.\n- Note whether `loadConfig` ever returns `{}` or defaults for bad JSON."}